    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d443">

                <pb facs="0629" n="539" sameAs="frus:frus1934v03/pg539"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">701.6593/20</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-09-28">September 28, 1934</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The Chinese Minister called and referred to the fact that Italy had proposed to
                    raise the rank of Minister to China to that of Ambassador, and inquired whether
                    this Government would be likewise disposed. He stated that Secretary
                        Kellogg<anchor n="81" xml:id="d443-f1"/> on a former occasion had sounded
                        <pb facs="0630" n="540" xml:id="pg540"/> out other governments<anchor n="82"
                        xml:id="d443-f2"/> to ascertain whether they would be disposed to raise this
                    rank to that of Ambassador, but that for some reason the matter failed. He
                    indicated genuine interest in favorable action.</p>
                <p>I replied that I was surprised to learn about the Italian movement, which I only
                    knew about through the public print.<anchor n="83" xml:id="d443-f3"/> I added
                    that I would give attention to his request.</p>
                <closer><signed>C[<hi rendition="#small-caps">ordell</hi>] H[<hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">ull</hi>]</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d443-f1" n="81" xml:id="d443-f1-content">
                <p>Frank B. Kellogg, Secretary of State, 1925–29.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d443-f2" n="82" xml:id="d443-f2-content">
                <p>See <ref target="frus1928v02#pg_201"><hi rendition="#italic">Foreign
                            Relations</hi>, 1928, vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">ii</hi>, pp.
                        201</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d443-f3" n="83" xml:id="d443-f3-content">
                <p>Notations on the original documents do not indicate that the previous
                    correspondence on this subject had been routed to the Secretary’s office. On
                    outgoing telegrams the Secretary’s name was initialed by the Under Secretary of
                    State, William Phillips.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
